  "title": "Doppelganger — Full Collection",
  "summary": "In 1995, the author found a truer self in 3 a.m. IRC chats, questioning if that digital self still exists. Decades later, we've fed fragments into databases, creating digital clones that represent us in transactions and communication. This raises questions: Are our digital selves more important than physical? How do our selves engage differently? Are assets and labor digital-only?\n\nDavid Rudnick started <a href=\"https://twitter.com/David_Rudnick/status/1372157141096787976?s=20&t=gWwUEb4TgFuIXjO9wShCMA\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">describing</a> some of these issues as an existential struggle for primacy: “Physical prime: an ideology in which value + meaning only exist to the extent they can be traced to a physical source” and “Digital prime: an ideology in which value + meaning only exist to the extent they can be traced to a digital source.” These extreme ideologies are not viable; we must find balance, embracing both digital and physical selves.\n\nA key difference of our digital selves is our lack of control. They are effectively us, yet constructed and presented by and for others – our doppelgangers. German for “double-walker,” doppelgangers originally foretold bad luck, later referring to perfect twins with oppositional intentions. More recently, the term identifies look-alikes across social media due to similar digital photos.\n\nOur modern doppelgangers fuse these ideas: ethereal yet more real, shaped by algorithms and ad research. They are not us, but represent us in most modern online interactions.\n\nThe works in *Doppelganger* explore identities that cross and fuse boundaries, with artists defining the self in connection with technological change. Each work exists in both digital and physical forms, offering different understandings through materiality or its absence, hoping for balance and reconciliation.",
